.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1238 AM PDT Mon Jul 27 2026
(Today and tonight)

Satellite imagery tonight continues to indicate marine stratus
struggling to make a push inland. The SFO-SAC pressure gradient is
around +4 which should result in an inland push by early morning,
and clearing by late morning, possibly early afternoon for the
terminals closer to the coast. Temperatures today are generally
right around normal for this time of year, warmer than normal at
higher elevations (~1500ft and higher). Breezy onshore flow
especially into the gaps and valleys across our area will continue
to dry fuels (see fire weather discussion for more details) and lead
to elevated fire weather concerns, especially at higher elevations
that will not see good RH recovery overnight. The most dangerous
portion of the forecast both today and through the remainder of the
week is the S-SW swell producing sneaker waves and riptides. One
count had over 25 water rescues across our Pacific coastline Sunday,
and likely more that didn`t get reported to the media. Be extra
careful and never turn your back on the water.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1238 AM PDT Mon Jul 27 2026
(Tuesday through Sunday)

Warmer temperatures are on the way, especially by the end of the
week into next weekend with increasing odds of more widespread
Moderate HeatRisk and potential heat advisories. However, there`s
still plenty uncertainty in the magnitude of the increasing heat.
Cluster analysis maintains some troughing in our vicinity until late
next weekend into the first week of August. Deterministic guidance
struggled with Fausto this far out and expect that to continue with
Genevieve. Depending how far north Genevieve tracks will determine
how far north the persistent troughing over the EPAC gets nudged
north and allows more impactful H50 height rises and thickness
increases. The forecast remains dry throughout with the exception of
possible coastal drizzle associated with the marine layer. Fire
weather concerns will continue to increase through the extended
forecast as fuels continue to dry and cure. Avoid any activities
that could lead to a spark and ignition, fires that do start are
likely to spread quickly.

