.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 534 AM PDT Mon Jul 27 2026

Breezy onshore winds and critically dry afternoon RH through the
week will translate to an elevated fire weather threat. This threat
will persist through mid-week and potentially into the latter half
of the week and into the weekend. The most likely region for starts
and fire spread will be across the interior Central Coast where
Energy Release Components (ERC) are projected to climb above the
95th percentile. While morning RH recovery will moderate some
through the week, the shallow marine layer will equate to generally
poor overnight recovery for elevations at or above 2,000 feet
through Wednesday morning. This will be particularly true across the
Mayacamas, highest peaks of the East Bay Hills, the Diablo Range,
Santa Cruz Mountains, Santa Lucia and Gabilan Ranges. Nocturnal
drying (rapid decrease in RH through the pre-danw hours) may also
transpire which will increase the burn period or time in which fire
behavior may not diminish. Individuals across the area are advised
to avoid activities that may spark a fire. Fire crews are urged to
exercise extra caution when executing any prescribed burns,
especially where fuels, topography, and weather align.

