.BEACHES...
Issued at 145 PM PDT Sun Jul 26 2026

Tropical cyclones in the Eastern Pacific will continue to
generate 18-20 second (long-period) southwesterly swell which has
reached the coastline of the San Francisco Bay Area and Central
Coast. As such, sneaker waves and strong rip currents are the
primary concerns. However, breaking waves can reach up to 10 feet
along some south and southwest facing beaches and break points
(like Santa Cruz, Pacifica, and parts of Stinson or Ocean Beach).
This can be particularly surprising when wave heights increase
suddenly after a period of seemingly tranquil wave action.
In fact, sneaker waves can have 20+ minutes in between the larger
sets (breaking waves) with significant "flat/calm looking" times
between them. Due to the long period, sneaker waves are powerful
and can catch beachgoers by surprise and can result in people and
pets being swept into the cold, turbulent ocean. Rip currents are
strong enough to pull the strongest swimmers away from shore.
Check conditions before you head out! Swim near a lifeguard if
possible. Stay back from the beach, and away from jetties, piers,
beachside rocks, and other waterside infrastructure. Never turn
your back to the ocean and if you have small children or pets at
the beach, please keep an eye on them. Hazardous beach conditions
are anticipated to persist through Wednesday afternoon.

